It's supposed to be
Peter's out.

00:14:22.033 --> 00:14:23.501
And everybody's like,

00:14:23.501 --> 00:14:26.968
"It's not Peter's off.
It's Peter's out."

00:14:26.968 --> 00:14:30.200
But what I was
trying to say was "[Bleep] off."

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.601
So the Peter part
was the -- where my error.

00:14:36.167 --> 00:14:39.901
I showed a really cool letter
last week from E.B. White,

00:14:39.901 --> 00:14:42.434
a letter no one
had ever seen before.

00:14:42.434 --> 00:14:45.501
And again, we've been
talking a fair amount

00:14:45.501 --> 00:14:47.734
about E.B. White.

00:14:47.734 --> 00:14:52.200
And boy, oh, boy,
did I hear it from you, jackals,

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:53.534
to the point that I'm not
even sure

00:14:53.534 --> 00:14:56.234
it's worth sharing things
with you guys anymore.

00:14:56.234 --> 00:14:59.868
So, you know,
it's this really cool letter

00:14:59.868 --> 00:15:02.267
from 1953 and --

00:15:02.267 --> 00:15:05.133
and a lot of you said
E.B. Write --

00:15:05.133 --> 00:15:08.133
E.B. White, an accomplished
what writer like E.B. White

00:15:08.133 --> 00:15:11.834
would never have
written the sentence,

00:15:11.834 --> 00:15:17.167
"Is it [bleep] up that
two humans has a kid mouse?"

00:15:19.334 --> 00:15:22.834
But they were like, obviously
the correct grammar is had.

00:15:22.834 --> 00:15:25.601
Well, I don't know.

00:15:25.601 --> 00:15:30.434
Maybe it had been a long time
since he used a typewriter.

00:15:30.434 --> 00:15:34.868
And maybe this was
the eighth try

00:15:34.868 --> 00:15:39.234
and this was the best one
by far.

00:15:39.234 --> 00:15:42.000
And maybe whatever his
equivalent of a producer was,

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:43.834
was banging on his office door
saying,

00:15:43.834 --> 00:15:47.234
"We got to go."

00:15:47.234 --> 00:15:49.567
I don't know. I'm not him.

00:15:52.901 --> 00:15:54.567
Maybe he had to be like,
this is, you know what,

00:15:54.567 --> 00:15:56.634
this is good as
it's going to be today.

00:15:56.634 --> 00:16:00.501
But let's let the man rest.

00:16:00.501 --> 00:16:03.167
Why are you criticizing E.B.?
It's E.B. White.

00:16:03.167 --> 00:16:06.200
Look at the date. We've talked
about that, right up there.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:09.901
And then everybody was like,
oh, why did E.B. White

00:16:09.901 --> 00:16:13.901
use a capital I for one
instead of a lowercase L?

00:16:13.901 --> 00:16:15.200
I don't know.

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:19.234
Why doesn't
a typewriter have a one?

00:16:19.234 --> 00:16:22.767
Why is it some weird riddle to
know where you're supposed to --

00:16:22.767 --> 00:16:24.601
he's got all the other numbers.

00:16:27.601 --> 00:16:29.801
I don't know.

00:16:29.801 --> 00:16:32.567
Someone else said if you wanted
to make the letter look older,

00:16:32.567 --> 00:16:34.501
you should have
dipped it in tea.

00:16:34.501 --> 00:16:36.934
I -- Why are you telling me?

00:16:36.934 --> 00:16:39.667
I didn't do this.

00:16:39.667 --> 00:16:45.300
Look, it's signed by
E.B. White. Okay?

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:46.634
And I was so upset by this

00:16:46.634 --> 00:16:51.167
that I reached out to
the Cornell University Library

00:16:51.167 --> 00:16:53.167
which houses
the E.B. White Archive

00:16:53.167 --> 00:16:55.400
it just so happens.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:59.067
And, well, I unearthed
another letter.

00:17:02.434 --> 00:17:06.567
And, you know, again,

00:17:06.567 --> 00:17:07.868
very hesitant to share it
with you

00:17:07.868 --> 00:17:09.667
after you guys were such jerks
about the last one.

00:17:09.667 --> 00:17:13.033
But here we go.

00:17:13.033 --> 00:17:15.734
[ Speaks indistinctly ]

00:17:15.734 --> 00:17:17.901
March 25th, so one day later,

00:17:17.901 --> 00:17:21.634
and it looks like he did get it
right this time. Okay.

00:17:21.634 --> 00:17:24.667
"Dear Kaye, wanted to circle
back regarding my last letter.

00:17:24.667 --> 00:17:26.567
I'm realizing now there were
quite a few typos

00:17:26.567 --> 00:17:27.934
and grammatical errors.

00:17:27.934 --> 00:17:29.467
The fact is, I'm under
a lot of pressure lately.

00:17:29.467 --> 00:17:33.567
My typewriter doesn't have a one
button, and it's driving --

00:17:36.033 --> 00:17:37.434
It's driving me cuckoo.

00:17:37.434 --> 00:17:39.200
Plus it's starting to dawn on me
that you're right.

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:40.834
And the ending
to 'Stuart Little' does suck."

00:17:40.834 --> 00:17:44.367
Okay, pretty big deal.

00:17:44.367 --> 00:17:45.634
"I called my publisher
and begged them

00:17:45.634 --> 00:17:46.734
to let me add a proper ending,

00:17:46.734 --> 00:17:48.567
but they're being
total dicks about it.

00:17:48.567 --> 00:17:50.667
O.M.G. complain much, E.B.?

00:17:55.667 --> 00:17:56.767
Thanks for letting me unload.

00:17:56.767 --> 00:17:57.901
All the best
to your future endeavors.

00:17:57.901 --> 00:17:59.501
Sincerely, E.B. White."

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:04.767
I mean, oh, and that's cool.

00:18:04.767 --> 00:18:07.300
"P.S., if this letter
looks older than the last one,

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:08.934
it's because
I dipped it in tea."

00:18:14.133 --> 00:18:17.000
All right. Well,

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:20.667
we're really happy to have
Shoemaker back.

00:18:20.667 --> 00:18:22.267
Happy St. Patrick's Day.

00:18:24.634 --> 00:18:26.267
Pad it out.

00:18:32.234 --> 00:18:33.834
Because...

00:18:38.734 --> 00:18:40.634
One more thing
I have to show you.

00:18:53.801 --> 00:18:55.601
I actually don't think
there is.

